          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-body">
        <div class="container article-shell">
          <div class="prose pillar-body">
            <p class="prose-lede">Home publishing splits into four tiers, and where you can land a byline depends on whether you have real projects or just opinions.</p>

            <p>The shelter magazines do not take guest posts. The DIY and improvement brands take how-tos from experienced trades. The decor blogs are open. The real-estate platforms want data. The list of 100 is below, grouped by tier.</p>

            <h2 id="tier">Pick your tier first</h2>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Tier</th><th>Examples</th><th>How you get in</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>Shelter magazines</td><td>Architectural Digest, House Beautiful, Elle Decor, Dwell, Livingetc</td><td>Not guest posts. A contribution from a working designer or architect with a portfolio</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>DIY &amp; improvement brands</td><td>This Old House, Bob Vila, Family Handyman, The Spruce, Bower &amp; Branch</td><td>How-tos from experienced trades and makers, with step-by-step images or video</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Decor &amp; design blogs</td><td>Apartment Therapy, Remodelista, Design Milk, Hooked on Houses, Young House Love</td><td>Open to contributions; real projects, before-and-after, styling with your own photos</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Real estate &amp; home services</td><td>Zillow, Realtor.com, Homes.com, Angi, HomeAdvisor, HousingWire</td><td>Market data, cost breakdowns, buyer and renovation analysis</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <h2 id="metrics">What the metrics mean</h2>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Metric</th><th>What it is</th><th>How much it matters</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>Domain Rating (DR)</td><td>Ahrefs' 0 to 100 score for a whole site's backlink profile</td><td>A useful filter, not a verdict</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Domain Authority (DA)</td><td>Moz's equivalent 0 to 100 score</td><td>Same idea as DR</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Page Authority (PA)</td><td>Moz's score for one specific page</td><td>Matters once you know which page your post will live on</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>URL Rating (UR)</td><td>Ahrefs' score for one specific URL</td><td>Same purpose as PA</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Monthly Traffic</td><td>Estimated organic visits per month</td><td>The most honest signal that a link will be seen</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Here is the deal:</strong> home content lives or dies on the imagery. A placement on a site whose readers are mid-renovation, with your own project photos, is worth more than a link from a general lifestyle site that runs stock.</p></div>

            <h2 id="list">The 100 home sites</h2>
            <p>Domain Rating, Domain Authority, Page Authority, URL Rating and estimated monthly traffic. Every domain was checked live in September 2026; shut-down and merged sites were swapped for live equivalents. Metrics are third-party estimates and shift over time.</p>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>#</th><th>Website</th><th>DR</th><th>DA</th><th>PA</th><th>UR</th><th>Monthly traffic</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  {SITES.map((s) => (
                    <tr>
                      <td>{s.n}</td>
                      <td>{s.site}</td>
                      <td>{s.dr}</td>
                      <td>{s.da}</td>
                      <td>{s.pa}</td>
                      <td>{s.ur}</td>
                      <td>{s.traffic}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <h2 id="pitch">The pitch that works for home editors</h2>
            <ol>
              <li><strong>Lead with the project or the data.</strong> "We renovated a 1930s galley kitchen for GBP 9k, here is the breakdown" earns the read.</li>
              <li><strong>Bring your own photography.</strong> Wide shots, detail shots, before-and-after. Home editors will not run a project without real images.</li>
              <li><strong>Pitch the section.</strong> "For your small-space coverage" or "for the budget-reno series" shows you read the site.</li>
              <li><strong>Match the format.</strong> Magazines want a finished project story. Improvement brands want a clear how-to. Real-estate platforms want a data piece.</li>
              <li><strong>Bring the assets.</strong> A source list, a cost table, a floor plan, a materials breakdown.</li>
              <li><strong>Disclose any brand, product or client</strong> involved in the project.</li>
            </ol>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>The test:</strong> would an editor run this if you sold nothing? If yes, pitch it. If it is a styled shot of your product range, it goes in the bin.</p></div>

            <h2 id="mistakes">What not to do</h2>
            <ul class="check-list">
              <li>Pitch a "top 10 paint colours 2026" listicle. Every site has a rolling one.</li>
              <li>Submit a project with renders instead of photos of the finished space.</li>
              <li>Send the same pitch to a shelter magazine and a budget-DIY blog.</li>
              <li>Stuff affiliate links, or point exact-match anchors ("best cordless drill") at your store.</li>
              <li>Use AI-generated decor copy with no real project behind it.</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> a few placements on sites whose readers are actively renovating, with your own project imagery, will do more for rankings and leads than a long list of thin home-blog links. Use this list to find your few.</p></div>
          </div>

